  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Victor Vargas is a Venezuelan banker who founded Banco Barinas and acquired Banco Occidental de Descuento. A law graduate of Andrés Bello Catholic University, he expanded BOD through further acquisitions and became a regional banking leader. The bank's Venezuelan business later transferred assets and liabilities to Banco Nacional de Crédito.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Independent career accounts and the acquiring bank establish the historical sequence."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Victor Vargas is a Venezuelan banker who founded Banco Barinas and acquired Banco Occidental de Descuento. A law graduate of Andrés Bello Catholic University, he expanded BOD through further acquisitions and became a regional banking leader. The bank's Venezuelan business later transferred assets and liabilities to Banco Nacional de Crédito.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "At a polo final in Cap Cana in 2016, Victor Vargas scored the goal that secured victory for Lechuza Caracas, the team he owned. The Venezuelan banker was also a partner in the Dominican Republic's Polo Challenge. He wanted to expand the local playing infrastructure, announcing additional fields at his club and encouraging other venues to follow.\n\nHis participation extended to the European circuit. At Sotogrande the following year, he played alongside James Harper, David Stirling and Juan Martín Nero as Lechuza won the Silver Cup. Vargas received the final's best-player award, with Stirling crediting his goals as an important part of the team's performance.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Firsthand event report and primary tournament records document sustained ownership and participation. Announced expansion remains a plan."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S6",
      "S7",
      "S8"
    ],
    "word_count": 105,
    "html": "<p>At a polo final in Cap Cana in 2016, Victor Vargas scored the goal that secured victory for Lechuza Caracas, the team he owned. The Venezuelan banker was also a partner in the Dominican Republic's Polo Challenge. He wanted to expand the local playing infrastructure, announcing additional fields at his club and encouraging other venues to follow.</p>\r\n<p>His participation extended to the European circuit. At Sotogrande the following year, he played alongside James Harper, David Stirling and Juan Martín Nero as Lechuza won the Silver Cup. Vargas received the final's best-player award, with Stirling crediting his goals as an important part of the team's performance.</p>\r\n"
  },
